# type State = { a: number; b: number };
# function widen(state: State, c: number) { return { ...state, c }; }
# As walk/typed-operations/spread-of-a-layout-is-an-allocation-and-one-copy-of-its-fields.ir, but the layout of the literal declares `c` in front of `a` and `b`, and no allocation lists `a`, `b`.
# The object gets the keys of `state` in the order in which `state` got them, and an allocation creates only a row of fields whose order the image holds. The summary says what is copied into what,
# and Link says what is expected of the parameter: the plan takes each order of a State that lists all its fields as an order of the literal's layout.
# Without it the walk makes the object empty and defines `a` and `b` by name, by a closed operation each.
# RUN: plan orders mechanisms=all
